<% content_for(:title) { "Features — Dotted" } %>

<div class="features--container">


  <article class="features--card" data-colour="1">
    <h2>Rapid logging</h2>
    <p>
      A chat-style dock at the bottom of your timeline: type the thought, or
      attach a file. No nested “create inside X” maze.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="2">
    <h2>Two bullets, one flow</h2>
    <p>
      Text and attachments share one composer, and every bullet can be
      checked off when it is done.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="3">
    <h2>One infinite daylog</h2>
    <p>
      Everything you write lands in a single feed. Recent days are their own
      sections; older ones settle into Last week, Last month, and beyond.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="4">
    <h2>Upcoming</h2>
    <p>
      Schedule a bullet for a later day and it waits on the Upcoming screen,
      then joins your daylog when its day comes.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="6">
    <h2>Capture, then curate</h2>
    <p>
      Brain-dump first, organise later. The daylog and collections
      keep the noise navigable without slowing you down.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="7">
    <h2>Move, don’t rewrite</h2>
    <p>
      Later, Save, Archive, Complete — move bullets instead of rewriting them,
      all from one selection menu.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="8">
    <h2>Keyboard-first</h2>
    <p>
      Every common intent — compose, navigate, pop, collect, complete, archive —
      has a hotkey, with hints surfaced inline.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="2">
    <h2>Postpone without guilt</h2>
    <p>
      Push a bullet to tomorrow — or any day — and keep going. Decisions
      deferred, not lost.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="3">
    <h2>Collections as notebooks</h2>
    <p>
      Named, coloured, icon’d collections. Save bullets from the daylog,
      then export a collection as HTML when you want it out.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="6">
    <h2>Archive soft, purge later</h2>
    <p>
      Soft-hide what’s done, browse archives when you need them, and let
      retention clean expired items after about thirty days.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="8">
    <h2>Quick search</h2>
    <p>
      Press Cmd/Ctrl + K to find bullets and collections fast without
      leaving the keyboard.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="1">
    <h2>Publish what you want</h2>
    <p>
      Publish individual bullets to share them on the internet. Each one gets a
      short code and a clean public view — no login to read.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="2">
    <h2>Appearance that stays quiet</h2>
    <p>
      Home tint themes — default, warm, cool, nature, cheese. Atmosphere without
      a settings rabbit hole.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="3">
    <h2>Outlining</h2>
    <p>
      Like TaskPaper — but no special headers. Any bullet can outline others:
      just indent and the tree builds itself. Navigate it with the keyboard,
      fold the noise away, focus on one branch.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="4">
    <h2>CLI &amp; agent integration</h2>
    <p>
      Drive Dotted from a terminal or from an AI agent. The same bullets
      and collections — programmable from outside the browser.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="5">
    <h2>Hooks</h2>
    <p>
      Drop bullets onto your daylog from Zapier, agents, or your own
      scripts — one URL per integration.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="6">
    <h2>Self-host, open source</h2>
    <p>
      Ready for self-deployment with Kamal. Your data stays in your own SQLite
      databases — no Redis, no telemetry. Self-host freely under the O’Saasy
      license.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="features--card" data-colour="7">
    <h2>Crafted with love</h2>
    <p>
      Built on Rails 8 and Hotwire, SQLite and Solid Queue under the hood,
      Kamal to ship it.
    </p>
  </article>
</div>
